```java
import java.util.*;

public class TestClass {

    private static Map<String, List<Attr>> attrs = new HashMap<>();

    public void addAttribute(String key, Attr attr) {
        if (!attrs.containsKey(key)) {
            attrs.put(key, new ArrayList<>());
        }
        attrs.get(key).add(attr);
    }

    public String getAttr(String key) {
        return attrs.getOrDefault(key, new ArrayList<>()).get(0).getValue();
    }

    public List<Attr> getValueList(String key) {
        return attrs.getOrDefault(key, new ArrayList<>());
    }

    public void setAttribute(String key, Attr attr) {
        if (attrs.containsKey(key)) {
            attrs.get(key).add(attr);
        } else {
            addAttribute(key, attr);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TestClass testClass = new TestClass();

        List<Attr> list1 = new ArrayList<>();
        Attr attr1 = new Attr("key1", "value1");
        list1.add(attr1);

        List<Attr> list2 = new ArrayList<>();
        Attr attr2 = new Attr("key2", "value2");
        list2.add(attr2);

        testClass.setAttribute("key1", list1);
        testClass.setAttribute("key2", list2);

        System.out.println(testClass.getAttr("key1").getValue()); // prints "value1"
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(list1)); 
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(list2));
    }
}
```

This Java code simulates a simple data storage system that allows for adding, retrieving, and updating attributes stored in an array of `Attr` objects. The `TestClass` class serves as the central hub, while the `Attr` class represents individual attribute values with their respective keys.

The `TestClass` class has methods to add attributes to the system, retrieve specific attributes, and store lists of attributes.

In the `main()` method, a test class is created with an initial set of attributes. Then, two lists are added to the system, and the system is updated to reflect these changes. Finally, the system is queried for values associated with certain keys, demonstrating its functionality.
